.TH CMEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-10-30" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(buffer, buf_size, elem_size,
                                         fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
                                         single_threaded, err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool on top of a buffer that the caller provides, so
the pool takes no memory from the heap for its entries. Fallback
entries, when the fallback is on, come from the standard malloc and
free.

It is the same as calling cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp()
with NULL memory management procedures, and every rule of that page
applies.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                     Pointer to the preallocated buffer
buf_size                   Size of the buffer in bytes
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes. It must be
                           > 0. If it is smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t),
                           it is rounded up. The maximum is SIZE_MAX
                           less the stride rounding
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from the heap when the pool
                           is empty
single_threaded            If true, take no lock
err                        Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Declare the buffer with CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER(), which
sizes and aligns it for the pool. The caller must destroy the pool with
cmempool_destroy() before the buffer goes out of scope.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER (3),
.BR cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp (3),
.BR cmempool_create (3),
.BR cmempool_destroy (3)
